@page "/build"
@implements IDisposable
@inject CharacterSession Session
@inject AccountCampaignStore Campaigns

<PageTitle>Tier — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

<h1>Choose a tier</h1>
<p class="muted">The tier sets the Hero Point budget and the Trait Cap.</p>

@* **The two sample characters used to be here and are now in the portfolio.** They are a
   demonstration — a way to see what a finished sheet looks like without building one — and a
   demonstration is not a step in making your own character. Somebody who has come here to build
   one had to walk past them first.

   **And "Starting over" went the same way.** It was the first thing on this page: one red button
   and a link away to somebody else's finished character. That is a strange greeting for a visitor
   who arrived to build one — what belongs at the top is what they already have, which is what
   `CharacterManager` shows. Discarding is still here; it is a row's own control now, plus the same
   "Start a new character" it always was. *@
<CharacterManager />

@* **One line, and deliberately not a second budget strip.** The running total lives on the strip
   above and stays there; what this says is where the tier came from, which is the one thing the
   cards below cannot say for themselves. A panel here would be the fourth band of chrome on the
   page a visitor meets first.

   **A disagreement reads as information, not as an error.** These campaigns climb tiers in play,
   so a character built to one tier in a game played at another is ordinary traffic — and nothing
   is repaired either way, because raising the tier turns an illegal character legal in silence and
   lowering it moves Resolve. *@
@if (_campaign is { } campaign)
{
    <p class="small muted">@CampaignLine(campaign)</p>
}

@* Six cards rather than six full-width rows. These are the one set of choices on the
   whole app a reader is meant to compare against each other rather than search through,
   which is why this list alone turns its filter box off — and why each card carries its
   consequence, the budget and the cap, as a line of its own under the description. *@
<OptionList Class="cards" Filterable="false">
    @foreach (var tier in Session.Rules.Tiers)
    {
        var tierId = tier.Id;
        <OptionRow Name="@tier.Name"
                   Caveat="@Consequence(tier)"
                   Cost="@($"{tier.HeroPoints} Hero Points · Trait Cap {tier.TraitCapRank}d")"
                   Selected="@(Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId == tierId)"
                   OnSelect="() => SelectTier(tierId)">
            <NameExtra>
                @if (Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId == tierId)
                {
                    <span class="tag own">Selected</span>
                }
            </NameExtra>
        </OptionRow>
    }
</OptionList>

@* The sandbox, put where the budget is introduced rather than in the chrome — the tier is what
   sets the figure, so this is where switching it off makes sense.

   **Two cards in the grid's idiom above, as their own group under a rule.** It was one button in
   a full-width panel, and the chrome — a heading, a rule and an "Optional" aside — outweighed the
   control about four to one.

   **They are not a seventh and eighth tier card.** A tier is a pick-one-of-six and this is an
   orthogonal boolean, and you still pick a tier without a limit because the Trait Cap still
   applies. So it is its own two-option group: the same idiom, separated by a rule and a heading
   that say this is a different question.

   **And the pair fixes a real defect the single button had.** That button's label flipped between
   naming the action and naming the state — off it read "Hold me to the tier's budget", which is
   what pressing it would do, and on it read "Building without a limit", which is what was already
   happening. A glance could not tell which of the two it was reporting. Two cards, both always
   visible and exactly one selected, cannot be ambiguous that way: each names a state, and the
   selected one is the state the character is in.

   **It is not the Hero/Villain switch and must not become one again.** Ch.9 builds Villains by
   exactly the Hero rules, so "no budget" was never a fact about Villains: it is a GM building to
   whatever the scene needs, which a Hero campaign does too. Keeping them apart means a Villain
   can be held to a tier's points and a Hero need not be.

   **The caveats are printed rather than hung on a tip**, which is the rule a row that says its
   own piece already follows: the tip this replaced answered what somebody hesitates over here —
   whether switching the budget off stops the tool checking the character at all — and that
   sentence is now on the card it belongs to, where it is read without hovering for it. *@
<section class="budget-choice" aria-labelledby="budget-choice-heading">
    <h2 id="budget-choice-heading">Hero Point limit</h2>

    <OptionList Class="cards" Filterable="false">
        <OptionRow Name="Building to the tier's budget"
                   Caveat="Spending over the tier's Hero Points is reported as a finding."
                   Pressed="@(!Session.UnlimitedBudget)"
                   Selected="@(!Session.UnlimitedBudget)"
                   OnSelect="() => Session.UnlimitedBudget = false">
            <NameExtra>
                @if (!Session.UnlimitedBudget)
                {
                    <span class="tag own">Selected</span>
                }
            </NameExtra>
        </OptionRow>

        <OptionRow Name="Building without a limit"
                   Caveat="Only the points budget stops being enforced. Trait Caps, flaw limits and where a Pro or Con is allowed are all still checked."
                   Pressed="@Session.UnlimitedBudget"
                   Selected="@Session.UnlimitedBudget"
                   OnSelect="() => Session.UnlimitedBudget = true">
            <NameExtra>
                @if (Session.UnlimitedBudget)
                {
                    <span class="tag own">Selected</span>
                }
            </NameExtra>
        </OptionRow>
    </OptionList>
</section>

<Panel Title="Starting package" Aside="Optional">
    <p class="small muted">
        A package buys the ranks it grants, at a discount. Higher ranks cost the difference.
    </p>

    <ChosenList>
        <ChosenRow>
            <Body>
                <div><b>No package</b></div>
                <div class="small muted">Buy every rank yourself.</div>
            </Body>
            <Actions>
                <button type="button" class="btn small"
                        disabled="@(Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId is null)"
                        @onclick="() => SelectPackage(null)">
                    @(Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId is null ? "Current" : "Choose")
                </button>
            </Actions>
        </ChosenRow>
        @foreach (var pkg in Session.Rules.CreationRules.OptionalPackages)
        {
            var chosen = Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId == pkg.Id;
            <ChosenRow Cost="@($"{pkg.Cost} HP")">
                <Body>
                    <div>
                        <b>@pkg.Name</b>
                        <span class="muted small">
                            — @(pkg.AbilitiesRank)d in every Ability, @(pkg.TalentsRank)d in every Talent
                        </span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="small muted">@pkg.Description</div>
                </Body>
                <Actions>
                    <button type="button" class="btn small" disabled="@chosen"
                            @onclick="() => SelectPackage(pkg.Id)">
                        @(chosen ? "Current" : "Choose")
                    </button>
                </Actions>
            </ChosenRow>
        }
    </ChosenList>
</Panel>

<StepButtons Next="build/characteristics" NextLabel="Buy characteristics"
             NextDisabled="@(Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId is null)" />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The campaign this character belongs to, or null when it belongs to none — and null too when
    /// it names one this account does not hold, which is reported by the finding rather than here.
    /// </summary>
    private Campaign? _campaign;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which campaign this character is in, and what it took from it.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The tier's printed name, never its id</b> — the rule every finding in this
    /// application follows, because an id is not what a tier is called.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string CampaignLine(Campaign campaign)
    {
        var mine = Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId;
        var theirs = campaign.TierId;
        var named = campaign.Name is { Length: > 0 } ? campaign.Name : "a campaign";

        if (theirs is null) return $"In {named}, which sets no tier.";

        var theirName = Session.Rules.GetTier(theirs)?.Name ?? theirs;

        if (mine is null) return $"In {named}. Choose a tier, or take its {theirName}.";

        return string.Equals(mine, theirs, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? $"In {named}, played at {theirName} — the tier below."
            : $"In {named}, played at {theirName}. Nothing has been changed either way.";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Redraw when the character changes, because a control on this page can change it without
    /// this page being the one that did.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Nothing here subscribed, and it showed on the deployed site.</b>
    /// <see cref="CharacterManager"/> is a child of this page: pressing its "Start a new
    /// character" empties the sheet, and the child's own re-render redrew the list of characters
    /// and left every tier card above it stale. So the Standard card went on reading
    /// <em>Selected</em> while the engine held no tier at all, until the page was reloaded — the
    /// one thing on that screen a reader would act on, saying the opposite of the truth. Importing
    /// a character reaches the same gap from the other direction: it gives the sheet a tier, and
    /// no card would have shown it.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Every handler below already calls <c>NotifyChanged</c> for the shell's sake, so this
    /// costs one subscription and no new call sites — and the page reads its cards straight off
    /// the sheet, so a redraw is all it needs.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized() => Session.Changed += StateHasChanged;

    /// <summary>
    /// Resolve the campaign once, on the way in.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Not on every change to the character.</b> Resolving a campaign is a round trip, and
    /// this page redraws on every step of a rank; re-reading it per keystroke is the read-per-letter
    /// the character manager's own split exists to avoid. A campaign changes when somebody edits it
    /// on the campaign screen, which is a navigation away from here and back.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() =>
        _campaign = await Campaigns.ForAsync(Session.Sheet);

    public void Dispose() => Session.Changed -= StateHasChanged;

    /// <summary>
    /// What choosing this tier means, in one line.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The description alone, and the note deliberately not appended to it.</b> Iconic
    /// is the only tier that carries a note, and its description already says the thing the note
    /// exists to say — "The 200 HP listed is a bare minimum for this tier." Concatenating the two
    /// printed that point twice in one paragraph, and because the cards are an equal-height grid
    /// row, the five-line Iconic card left High Level and Legendary with about five lines of dead
    /// white above their cost rule. An earlier version of this comment claimed the note was
    /// needed or the card would state a budget the rulebook does not; the description states it.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string Consequence(TierModel tier) => tier.Description;

    private void SelectTier(string id)
    {
        Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId = id;
        Session.NotifyChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Applying a package sets every Ability and Talent it grants to its rank, unless the
    /// character has already bought higher. Removing one leaves those ranks alone — they
    /// are the character's now, and silently deleting a player's choices to undo a
    /// discount would lose work rather than save Hero Points.
    /// </summary>
    private void SelectPackage(string? id)
    {
        Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId = id;

        if (id is not null &&
            Session.Rules.CreationRules.OptionalPackages.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Id == id) is { } pkg)
        {
            foreach (var ability in Session.Rules.Abilities)
                if (Session.Sheet.GetAbilityRank(ability.Id) < pkg.AbilitiesRank)
                    Session.Sheet.AbilityRanks[ability.Id] = pkg.AbilitiesRank;

            foreach (var talent in Session.Rules.Talents)
                if (Session.Sheet.GetTalentRank(talent.Id) < pkg.TalentsRank)
                    Session.Sheet.TalentRanks[talent.Id] = pkg.TalentsRank;
        }

        Session.NotifyChanged();
    }
}
